.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Breakpoint IDs are assigned by the server and are monotonically
increasing within a session.
IDs are never reused, even after breakpoints are removed.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]\(dqcommand\(dq\f[R] and \f[CR]\(dqfunction\(dq\f[R] kinds use
substring matching (\f[CR]strstr\f[R]), not glob or regex patterns.
The pattern \f[CR]\(dqrm\(dq\f[R] matches
\f[CR]\(dqrm \-rf /tmp\(dq\f[R], \f[CR]\(dqecho rm\(dq\f[R], etc.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]condition\f[R] expression is evaluated in the bash interpreter
context at the time the breakpoint is hit.
Shell variables, functions, and built\-ins are available.
.IP \(bu 2
Multiple breakpoints can be set on the same line or pattern.
Each is tracked independently with its own hit count.
.IP \(bu 2
The debugger must be enabled before adding breakpoints.
Calling this function with the debugger disabled returns
\f[CR]BC_ERR_SERVER\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Thread safety: the client library is not thread\-safe.
